  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed breakdown of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n---\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). Also known as the laryngeal prominence, the Adam's apple sits right on top of the thyroid gland, so the area is fittingly called the thyroid cartilage.\n\n---\n\n### Do Women Have One Too?\n\nYes, women do have Adam's apples, but they are usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n---\n\n### Why Is It Bigger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Equal size at birth:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth and those assigned female at birth until puberty.\n\n2. **Testosterone drives growth:** In men, the production of testosterone causes the larynx to grow larger, resulting in a deeper voice and a more prominent Adam's apple.\n\n3. **Larger voice box = bigger bump:** Once puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) in males may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n4. **Women's growth is less significant:** Girls also undergo changes to their voice box during puberty, but the degree of laryngeal growth in females isn't as significant as in males, so most women don't have a visible Adam's apple.\n\n---\n\n### The Shape Difference Also Matters\n\nThere is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n---\n\n### Why Does It Matter (Voice)?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one.\n\n---\n\n### What Does It Actually Do?\n\nThe Adam's apple, in relation with the thyroid cartilage which forms it, helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords (which are located directly behind it).\n\nIn summary, it all comes down to testosterone during puberty \u2014 it causes the larynx to grow much more dramatically in males, resulting in a larger, more visible Adam's apple and a deeper voice.",
